tag example;

saved: ?*int = null;

stash() {
    saved = new int[1](1)
}

bridge() {
    stash();
    relay()
}

relay() {
    bridge()
}

main(): int {
    relay();
    0
}

/* expect compile {|
noregion_call_chain.l8:6:13

    saved = new int[1](1)
            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^

stash stores this allocation in global saved. If stash runs inside a
region, saved could retain a pointer to memory freed when that region
ends.

If this value should be retained, declare stash as noregion. Its callers
also need noregion. This prevents those functions from being called
inside a region.

If the allocation is only needed temporarily, keep it local instead of
storing it in longer-lived storage, and use it before its region ends.

bridge calls stash, so choosing noregion also requires it on bridge.

relay calls bridge, so choosing noregion also requires it on relay.

main calls relay, so choosing noregion also requires it on main.

|} */
